 "result_text": "`DateTime` objects now work in f-strings and `str.format`, the same way `datetime` does. I added a `__format__` method in `src/DateTime/DateTime.py`, right after `strftime`.\n\nA non-empty format spec is passed to `strftime`. An empty spec returns `str(self)`, so `f'{d}'` behaves as it did before.\n\nI checked it with a quick script. For a DateTime of `2022/05/26 05:45:00 UTC`:\n- `f'{d:%d.%m.%Y %H:%M}'` gives `26.05.2022 05:45`.\n- `f'{d}'` gives `2022/05/26 05:45:00 UTC`.\n- `'{:%Y}'.format(d)` gives `2022`.\n\nI didn't run the repo's test suite or the issue's exact `{now = :%-d.%-m.%Y %H:%M}` example. The `%-d` flag isn't part of Python's own `strftime`, so on this code path it depends on your platform's C library, as it does for `datetime`.",
